>
  <p>A robot mower may handle the back lawn neatly and still leave you carrying it through a gate to reach the front. A steep edge, poor satellite reception or a cutting height that does not suit your grass can matter more than the advertised lawn capacity.</p>
  <p>Start with a sketch of the yard and the work you want to reduce. Australian Home Collective’s guide explains what to check before choosing a mower; it is research-based guidance, with no claim of firsthand product testing.</p>

  <aside class="worksheet-shortcut" aria-label="Free yard assessment worksheet">
    <p><strong>Free robot mower yard-assessment sheet</strong><span>Record lawn areas, passages, slopes and questions for the supplier.</span></p>
    <a href="/downloads/robot-lawn-mower-yard-assessment.pdf" download>Download worksheet (PDF)</a>
  </aside>

  <h2>Measure the lawn, then check the available mowing time</h2>
  <p>Measure the grass to be cut, excluding the house, paving, pool and garden beds. Split irregular lawns into simple shapes, estimate each area in square metres and add them together. Record disconnected sections separately.</p>
  <p>A capacity figure is not necessarily the area covered on one charge. Check the operating hours, terrain and mowing pattern behind it. Charging and travelling between areas also take time. Ask whether the mower can maintain your measured lawn during the hours you will actually allow it to run, including peak growth.</p>
  <p>A household reserving afternoons for children and pets has a different schedule from an empty yard. Do not assume a mower rated exactly at your lawn area has spare capacity for those restrictions.</p>

  <h2>Trace every passage, gate and lawn edge</h2>
  <p>Walk the route from the charging station to every lawn section. Measure the narrowest usable passage, including turns, and compare it with the installation instructions. Mower body width alone does not establish passage clearance.</p>
  <p>Mark steps, raised paving, exposed roots, holes, sprinkler heads and low furniture. Ask how the model handles a separate lawn: automatic travel, another charging station or manual carrying may be required. “Multi-zone” does not mean it can cross steps or a closed gate.</p>
  <p>Check the border beside walls and garden beds. Some grass may still need trimming; an edge-cutting feature does not establish the result at every boundary. Keep required setbacks separate from the physical lawn edge on your sketch.</p>

  <figure class="yard-plan">
    <img src="/images/guides/robot-lawn-mower-buying-guide-australia.svg" alt="Yard plan marks edges and setbacks, a narrow passage behind the house, a tree canopy and a charging station with power. A path separates the front lawn." width="1200" height="675" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>Sketch the whole route, including the areas the mower cannot reach automatically. Illustrative only; confirm all clearances for the chosen model.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Measure slopes where the mower must turn</h2>
  <p>Check the steepest working area and the slope along boundaries, particularly near retaining walls. A model may have a lower permitted slope at its boundary than inside the lawn. Wet ground, ruts and abrupt changes in level also need assessment.</p>
  <p><strong>Percent gradient and degrees are different.</strong> A rise of 20 cm over a horizontal distance of 100 cm is a 20% gradient, approximately 11.3 degrees. Measure the horizontal run, not the distance along the sloping surface. If access is unsafe, have the site assessed.</p>
  <p>Copy the exact model’s limits and measuring method. A headline slope rating is not approval for a bank beside a drop or pond.</p>

  <h2>Choose navigation for the yard you have</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Boundary wire:</strong> A physical wire defines the working area. It needs installation and may need repairs or repositioning when garden beds change. Check any guide-wire requirements too.</li>
    <li><strong>Satellite navigation:</strong> RTK, or real-time kinematic positioning, uses correction information to refine satellite positioning. Trees and buildings can affect reception. Check whether the system needs a local reference station, internet coverage or an ongoing service.</li>
    <li><strong>Camera or LiDAR navigation:</strong> Cameras interpret images; LiDAR measures distances using laser light. Some systems combine these with satellite positioning. Ask how the exact model handles your shaded passages, lawn edges and obstacles.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>“Wireless” does not mean every model uses the same technology or needs no setup. Request a site assessment or demonstration in the difficult parts of your yard, and establish what happens if positioning or connectivity is lost.</p>

  <h2>Match cutting height to your grass</h2>
  <p>Identify the grass variety and suitable mowing height before comparing adjustment ranges. Buffalo, couch and kikuyu should not automatically receive the same setting. Our <a href="/guides/lawn-care-basics/">lawn care guide</a> helps you assess the lawn first.</p>
  <p>Many robot mowers maintain grass by cutting small amounts frequently and leaving fine clippings behind. Check how to prepare an overgrown lawn and adjust the schedule through growth and dormancy. Do not assume a robot will clear long, neglected grass or collect leaves.</p>

